window: two modes that open at a device's size, a rebuilt menu bar, and a user rotation that relaunches

cocoa_bridge.mm, window_policy.{h,cpp}. The window has two modes, picked from
the View menu (Visualizar): Celular (the per-app portrait preference) and Tablet
(the default one). MACAPK_WINDOW_SIZE_PORTRAIT / MACAPK_WINDOW_SIZE_DEFAULT =
<W>x<H> give each the content size it opens with -- at launch, on a rotation and
from View > Tamanho Padrão -- applied as the resize a window manager makes
(ApplyUserResize) chained onto the transition that picked the preference, so the
app is given one Configuration. Without it the portrait box is the display
turned on its side (500x800 on a 1280x800 budget). With MACAPK_RESOLUTION_UI=1
each mode stays resizable inside its own layout class (portrait content width
320..599pt, default at least 720x480; Android's phone/tablet line is 600dp),
never fullscreen, and View > Travar Tamanho da Janela pins the current size (off
unless <root>/.macapk/resolution says "locked"). MACAPK_DEBUG_AUTOORIENT drives
the menu's orientation items in an unattended run.

The menu bar is rebuilt: the app menu (Sobre, Proxy de Rede, Limpar Cache,
Ocultar, Ocultar Outros, Mostrar Todos, Encerrar), Visualizar, Rolagem (scroll speed, whose
inertia, auto-scroll: the settings 0016 reads, kept in the user defaults),
Navegar (Voltar, Cmd+[), Idioma, Janela. MACAPK_MENU_CONTROLS=0 hides the
game-controls menu; MACAPK_MENU_DUMP=1 prints the bar as text.
Limpar Cache does what Settings > Storage > Clear cache does on a device: the
contents of every package's cache/ and code_cache/ under the Android root (the
link out of the bundle is followed), after a dialog that states the size.
Measured with the app running: 3781 files, 298.4 MB -> 2 MB, the app kept going
(MACAPK_DEBUG_CLEAR_CACHE=<secs> for an unattended run).

Measured on Instagram 455: opens 412x915 (window 412x947), Cmd+R -> 1280x800 ->
412x915, each by one relaunch; boot 10/10.

MACAPK_ROTATE_RELAUNCH=1 (off by default): a rotation the USER asked for
relaunches the activities alive at that moment instead of reporting the change
to them, and drops the orientation the app requested for the old display
(window_policy ToggleUserPref, DisplayConfig.macapkNoteUserRotation,
Activity.macapkClearRecordRequest). An app that declares
orientation|screenSize handles the change in place on a device, but one that
derives its size class only in onCreate is left half-converted: Instagram 455
went tablet -> phone with the tab bar back and the feed still on tablet insets,
and could not leave portrait at all once it had requested it for a phone-sized
display ("preference -> default (with the app's request portrait): box
unchanged").

DisplayConfig / Resources.updateConfiguration: the display's fields belong to
the display. Mac.apk serves the Application and every Activity from one
Resources, so an app that replays a Configuration it captured earlier (a locale
wrapper) also replays that moment's screen size into the configuration the next
activity launches with; on a device the activity's Resources get the window's
override configuration applied over whatever the app passed. A Resources that
was following the committed display keeps its display fields when the incoming
configuration carries a display that was committed before and is gone.
Measured: relaunched on 1280x800dp, Instagram left the shared configuration at
the boot display's 500x800dp sw500 and laid the tablet window out as a phone
(bottom tab bar, media 1280 px wide), and the rotation back saw diff=0x0 and did
nothing. With the guard the log reads "app updateConfiguration replayed a past
display (500x800dp sw500); kept 1280x800dp sw800 land"; portrait -> landscape
comes up with the side rail and the centred feed, landscape -> portrait with the
tab bar and phone insets, both by relaunch (MACAPK_DEBUG_AUTOROTATE=30,60).

Activity.onConfigurationChanged failures now log where they stopped.

